{"name":"Am I Blue?","hex":"#417086","fetched":"2026-10-21","looked":{"definitions":[],"entries":[{"title":"Am I Blue?","blocks":[{"text":"\"Am I Blue?\" is a 1929 song composed by Harry Akst (music) and Grant Clarke (lyrics), and published by M. Witmark & Sons. It was featured in four films that year, most notably with Ethel Waters in the movie On With the Show. It has become a standard covered by numerous artists. As a work from 1929 with its copyright renewed, it entered the public domain on January 1, 2025."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Am_i_blue.jpg/330px-Am_i_blue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Am_I_Blue%3F","wikidata":"Q4738922"},{"title":"Am I Blue? (essay)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Am I Blue?\" is an autobiographical essay written by Alice Walker in 1986. It was first published in her non-fiction collection, Living By the Word: Selected Writings 1973-1987."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Am_I_Blue%3F_(essay)","wikidata":"Q138544138"},{"title":"I","blocks":[{"text":"I is the ninth letter and the third vowel letter of the Latin alphabet, used in the modern English alphabet, the alphabets of other western European languages and others worldwide. Its name in English is i, plural i's or is."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Latin_letter_I.svg/330px-Latin_letter_I.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I","wikidata":"Q9893"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Am_i_blue.jpg/500px-Am_i_blue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Am_i_blue.jpg/500px-Am_i_blue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sheet music cover, 1929","link":"https://en.wikipedia.